feat(hook): record work a run left when nobody saw it die (#288)

#290 retires a killed run when its parent session sees the kill. A crash, an
OOM, a usage limit, or a session dying with its children fires no hook at all,
so nothing said the work was there. Measured today: two subagents died on a
usage limit, left uncommitted work in this tree, and nothing recorded it.

scripts/hooks/orphan-sweep.sh asks the question from the NEXT session's start,
which by construction is not the session that died. A marker still on disk
plus a ledger state that is no longer `active` means a run whose stop nobody
ran; its uncommitted paths, taken from its own tool-call bodies, are written
as one `run_unobserved` record and reported on stderr. It only records: no run
is retired, nothing is signed, the tree is not written, and no clock is read.
`active` is left to the reaper's grace.

The report does not offer a signing command. Every run it records is no longer
active, and the signer refuses those (IP §6.2). Measured on its first real
use: the `innsegl-commit -r` line it used to print was refused. Who signs such
work is #269.

SessionStart calls it before observe_session, which exits early when the
deployment is down. A missing or failing sweep never fails the session. CI
runs the self-test as its own job.

Known limit: only Edit, Write and NotebookEdit bodies are read, so a path a run
wrote through Bash is not claimed.

The sweep and its self-test were written by run-5ec3d4949c76febf14d5e722a3e861e5,
which died with no stop event and made no commit; the sweep recorded its own
author. The SessionStart wiring, the CI job and the hint fix are this commit's.
Driven by OPS-106..108 in orphan-sweep-selftest.sh and OPS-109 in
shim-selftest.sh.

